;; What the model reads of each tool brainyard/emacs.clj defines: its description,
;; by tool id (brainyard.tool-words, byj-2e8.5). Read by the model, so a
;; resource (RFC-001).
{"emacs$status"
  "Which Emacs this session reaches and whether it is up: its server, Emacs's version, whether byj.el is loaded, and the project's buffers with which hold unsaved edits. The other emacs$ tools are offered only while it is up."
 "emacs$context"
  "What the person is looking at in Emacs: the file or buffer, the cursor's line and column, whether it has unsaved edits, the text they selected, the major mode, the files in the other windows, and in an org file the heading path and the src block at point (language, name, line, header arguments, body). Ask when they say \"this\", \"here\" or \"the selection\"."
 "emacs$show"
  "Show the person a place in Emacs: visit :file at :line in the window they are in, highlight :line to :end until the next show, and put :message in the echo area. Changes nothing but what is on their screen. Use it to point at what you are explaining."
 "emacs$eval"
  "Evaluate Emacs Lisp in the person's Emacs, every form in :code, lexically; the last value, printed. It runs as them, with everything their Emacs reaches, and holds their Emacs while it runs, so keep it quick and do only what was asked: try a command you are writing for them, or ask Emacs what no other emacs$ tool answers."
 "emacs$note"
  "Leave notes on lines in the person's Emacs, where they work: each at the end of its line, dimmed, and all of them listed in *byj-notes*, which M-g n / M-g p (next-error) walk. :kind says what it is — issue, hint, good or question. With :clear, take notes away instead: :file's, or all of them. Changes nothing in any file."
 "emacs$edit"
  "Change the person's buffer in Emacs: replace :old, which must be there exactly once, with :new, as one undo step. The buffer is left unsaved and the new text highlighted, so they see the change, keep it by saving, or undo it — their own unsaved edits stay as they were. Use it to show a change rather than make one behind them: write-file and update-file write the disk, under whatever they have not saved."
 "emacs$xref"
  "Where a name is defined, or with :references where it is used, as the person's Emacs knows it — their LSP server through eglot or lsp-mode, elisp, or a tags table — for the language of :file. Name it by :identifier (found on :line, or its first place in the file) or by :line and :column. Each place is file, line, column and the text Emacs shows for it; :backend says which answered, and null with a note when the file has none, which is not the same as nothing found. Read-only. More exact than grep where a server runs: it knows scope, overloads and imports."
 "emacs$diagnostics"
  "The errors and warnings the person's Emacs shows for :file now — flymake's (eglot's LSP diagnostics among them) or flycheck's — for the buffer as it stands, unsaved edits included: line, column, severity, text and checker. :pending names checkers that have not reported since the last change, so ask again after a moment; :backend null with a note means nothing checks the file in Emacs, not that it is clean. Read-only."
 "emacs$describe"
  "What the person's own Emacs says about itself, from the keymaps and modes of :file's buffer (else the one in front of them) — their init, Doom's or Spacemacs's, evil's states — not the Emacs you remember. Give one of: :key (\"C-c C-c\") → the command it runs there, or the keys after it when it is a prefix; :command → its keys, whether it is interactive and its doc's first line, or that this Emacs has no such function; :prefix (\"C-c\") → every key that continues it there and what each runs; :apropos (\"rename symbol\") → commands whose names have all the words, bound ones first, with their keys; :modes true → the major mode and the minor modes on. Check every key and command with it before telling the person one. Read-only."
 "emacs$habits"
  "Which commands the person uses in Emacs, as byj-observe-mode saw them — theirs to turn on (M-x byj-observe-mode), keeping command names in their Emacs's memory, never what they type: the commands run most with the keys they used and the keys bound; :runs, a small command run many times in a row (a better command replaces it); :mx-with-keys, commands run through M-x that have a key; and the tips given, each with :used-since, how often its command ran after it. :on false says it is off. With :tip (a command) and :why, record a tip you gave instead, so a later look can tell whether it was taken up."
 "emacs$suggest"
  "Put a command in front of the person in Emacs, in *byj-howto*: :why (what it does for them, one line), the command, its keys in the buffer they are in — looked up there, never your word for them — its doc line, and a button that runs it. They press the keys or the button; you do not run it. Refused for a command their Emacs does not have."
 "emacs$features"
  "What the person's Emacs has, to set it up or to say why something is missing: its version, init file and package archives; for each of :features (\"eglot\", \"cider\") whether it is loaded or loadable, for each of :packages whether it is installed, for each of :programs (\"clojure-lsp\") where it is on exec-path; with :file, that buffer's major mode, the language server eglot would start for it, whether that is found and whether eglot runs there. Read-only; ask it before suggesting an install or a setting."
 "emacs$lessons"
  "Lessons in using Emacs itself, for coaching (/emacs coach emacs: …): without :name, each lesson's name, title and summary; with :name, its steps — each a title and the commands that show it done — and its text. A lesson names commands, never keys: look each key up with emacs$describe."
 "emacs$org-outline"
  "An org file's structure as Emacs has it, unsaved edits included: its headings (level, title, line, TODO, tags) and src blocks — each block's name, language, first and last line, header arguments as written and :results as in force, the absolute file it tangles to (inherited :tangle included; null for none), the noweb references <<name>> in it, and whether it has results. Read it before editing a literate file: the org file is the source, and the files it tangles to are output."
 "emacs$org-tangle"
  "Tangle an org file — write each src block to the file its :tangle says — through org-babel in the person's Emacs; the files written. Every target is checked against the directories tools may write before anything is written. Refused while the org buffer holds unsaved edits, since org saves it first. Edit the org file's blocks, never the files they tangle to, then tangle, then build or test what it wrote."
 "emacs$org-execute"
  "Run one src block of an org file through org-babel in the person's Emacs, by its :name or by a :line inside it; its results go under it as #+RESULTS, as when the person runs it, and are returned with the value. The buffer is left unsaved unless :save. The block runs as the person, in their Emacs, which waits while it runs: a block that takes long holds their Emacs, and stopping it at the bound does not stop it. A language needs its babel backend set up there (shell and emacs-lisp always)."
 "emacs$read"
  "A file's text as Emacs has it — unsaved edits included, which read-file does not see — or lines :start to :end of it; a file no buffer visits is read from the disk and says :visited false. :modified says whether the buffer differs from the disk. Clipped to its start past {max-chars} characters."
